Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.127 --> 00:00:11.210
ANDY QIN: Hello, everyone.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:14.230
Welcome to APP 101 breakout.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:16.660
Today we'd like to
introduce Google Distributed

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.430
Cloud, Hosting Mode.

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:20.980
My name is Andy Qin,
I'm a product manager

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:22.000
in Google Cloud.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.353
I'm here with my colleague Ray.

00:00:24.353 --> 00:00:25.270
RAY COLLINE: Hi there.

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:26.140
I'm Ray Colline.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.660
And I'm a software engineer,
who works on Google Cloud.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:29.800
And it's great to be here.

00:00:32.457 --> 00:00:34.040
ANDY QIN: In this
session, we'll first

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.610
look at market pain points,
and the limitations of existing

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:38.660
solutions.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.450
And then we will
introduce Google solution

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:44.750
in terms of how it's
defined, and what's

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:47.480
the key streams and
the characteristics.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.970
And we will then dive
deep into the product

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:52.730
areas, like platform,
services, and support.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:02.720
Today the public clouds provide
amazing results and benefits.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.190
So it seems everything is
moving to public Cloud.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:08.530
But the reality is
that not all workloads

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:11.590
can move to public Cloud
for different reasons,

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:15.980
regulatory, IP protection,
sovereignty concerns.

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:18.290
Those workloads that
stayed private, it's

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:20.990
very challenging
for them to leverage

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:24.170
all the cool innovation
from Cloud technologies

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:27.140
to empower their business,
and, oftentimes, those

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:32.410
are very special and specific
part of their business.

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:36.910
As we in public clouds
the infrastructure

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:38.410
is quite transparent.

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:41.680
Users do not need to care about
the gory details of things

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.430
like hardware operation.

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:46.486
However, that's not the
case for private Cloud.

00:01:46.486 --> 00:01:48.980
In the legacy private
Cloud customers

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:50.480
still have to
spend a lot of time

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.720
managing their infrastructure.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:57.260
And most hobbyists pinpointed
that customers will end up

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.020
with a public Cloud
and private Cloud

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:02.990
with different development
models and toolings,

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:07.100
and the resulted inconsistency
impacts business agility.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.590
The market pain
points are very clear,

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.400
but if you look at the
existing solutions out

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:21.780
in the marketplace they didn't
address all those pain points.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.980
As we know, some products are
viewed on proprietary stack.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.790
There's no consistency between
that and the public Cloud.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:34.140
Applications developed in
private mode cannot be easily

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.700
ported to the public Cloud.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.640
It's very difficult
to achieve write once,

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.860
and run anywhere [INAUDIBLE].

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:45.280
Some private Cloud products
a very infrastructure service

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:46.120
oriented.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:50.470
No advanced Cloud innovations
like AI/ML services and data

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:52.450
analytics.

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:55.960
And the management
methodologies are very legacy.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:59.230
For example, the more
declarative policy paradigm

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:03.550
is quite common in public
Cloud, but most private

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:06.940
Cloud are still using
the legacy imperative

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:10.460
tools to match their systems.

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.590
Some private Cloud products
do meet most requirements.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.100
However, they require
connections back

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:20.220
to their Cloud region for the
quintupling to even function.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:23.370
This poses a huge challenge
for sensitive workloads,

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.790
especially in the
specific market segments,

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:29.301
like defense and
critical infrastructure.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.680
Google Cloud recognizes
those market pain points

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:39.540
and the clear gaps
in the marketplace.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:43.070
We are going to offer a
solution that can directly

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:45.860
solve those challenges.

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:50.560
The solution is called Google
Distributed Cloud Hosted Mode.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.060
There's three key things to
highlight on this solution.

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:55.960
First, it doesn't
require connections back

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:56.920
to public Cloud.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:02.495
All quintupling, data plane,
match plane can be local.

00:04:02.495 --> 00:04:03.870
It helps you meet
the sovereignty

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:06.730
and the regulatory requirements.

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:09.510
Second, it comes with
highly differentiated

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:13.650
advanced services, like
AI/ML and the data analytics.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:16.680
We believe those services
are critical to empower

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:20.560
the customer to stay
competitive in their own market.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:25.300
And the third, it leverages
open source and open API.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:28.210
This provides an automated
portability and software

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:32.680
survivability, which are super
important to both business

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:36.600
agility and the
sovereignty needs.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:38.190
At this particular
point in time,

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.950
the Google Distributed
Cloud Hosted Mode

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:43.020
is still a pre-GA product.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:46.050
We are working hard to bring
this to the market soon.

00:04:50.328 --> 00:04:51.870
Besides the three
key things, I think

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:54.570
it's important to look
at the characteristics

00:04:54.570 --> 00:05:00.400
and the key strengths of Google
Distributed Cloud Hosted Mode.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:05.570
The name Hosted Mode tells a
lot about location and control.

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:08.200
The solution can be deployed
at a customer designated data

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:12.670
center, even in the totally
air gapped environment.

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:15.250
This solution is not just
the infrastructure play,

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:18.460
it comes with Google advanced
services, like AI/ML.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:22.900
The power of those technologies
and Google's years of expertise

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:25.750
are no longer limited
to the public Cloud,

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:29.530
with Google Distributed
Cloud Hosted Mode,

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:34.520
customers can now enjoy
those on their own premise.

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:36.170
Another strength
worth highlighting

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:39.500
is that the entire
solution is flexible.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:43.820
It comes as pre-assembled
and preconfigured racks

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:47.360
and is delivered to your
data center as a service.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:50.540
It can scale from 1
rack, to a couple racks,

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:53.670
and to hundreds of racks.

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:56.130
It has local
quintupling built in,

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:59.790
so it covers all sovereignty
dimensions, data sovereignty,

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:04.390
operational sovereignty,
and software sovereignty.

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:05.980
While it's built
by Google Cloud,

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:08.620
so it inherits the Cloud DNA.

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:11.470
Open and the Modern are
the design philosophies

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:15.280
we follow when we
build the solution.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.940
We believe the solution
will meet the needs of many

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:20.580
industries, where they have
a sensitive workload and they

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:25.210
cannot move to the public Cloud,
but they still want to leverage

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:27.640
the modern innovation
from Cloud.

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:32.760
Public sector is a prime
example of those industries.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:34.740
With that, let me
invite my colleague

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.710
Ray to talk about what
exactly is in the solution

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:41.710
and what are the
key capabilities.

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:43.430
RAY COLLINE: Thank you, Andy.

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:46.160
We're going to get into a little
bit of the technology that

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:49.580
makes up Google distributed
Cloud Hosted Mode.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:54.630
And we'll start with the
lowest level, the hardware.

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:56.850
So at Google Distributed
Cloud Hosted Mode

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:59.910
is full racks of hardware
optimized to deliver

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:02.460
an air gapped Cloud experience.

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:04.080
These racks include
modern hardware

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:07.440
from top OEM providers,
including HPE, NetApp,

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:09.240
and Cisco.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.220
All of the
infrastructure management

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:14.250
is taken care of by
Google Distributed Cloud.

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:16.650
These are things like
operating system upgrades,

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:21.510
firmware upgrades, switch OS
upgrades, NetApp upgrades,

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:22.350
et cetera.

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:26.040
All are taken care of as part
of Google Distributed Cloud.

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:30.690
It also includes GPUs
for AI/ML workloads.

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:34.770
And it includes storage block--

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:37.830
or sorry, block, file,
and object storage

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:40.120
in a redundant manner.

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:42.360
It also includes high
performance networking.

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:45.180
And in fact, each node
has full bandwidth

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:49.950
to all the other nodes
within a GDC instance,

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:55.480
and these instances can
be up to 10,000 VCPUs.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:58.840
In addition, it's
Kubernetes all the way down.

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:02.380
So Andy spoke earlier
about it being open, right?

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:05.710
We've taken that and we've
introduced Kubernetes APIs

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:09.370
all the way down into the
infrastructure management layer

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:12.850
so that the notion
of an OS upgrade

00:08:12.850 --> 00:08:16.960
is actually controlled through
the reliability of Kubernetes.

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:25.120
Google Distributed
Cloud Hosted Mode

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:28.450
is a full air gapped
Cloud solution stand alone

00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:30.850
from any other Cloud,
and so it comes

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:34.289
with sophisticated
multi-tendency.

00:08:34.289 --> 00:08:38.100
As we know in public
clouds things like GCP

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:41.400
have the notion of
organizations and projects,

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:45.390
and one creates a project
before doing anything on GPC,

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:48.150
creating a VM, creating a
cluster, creating a database.

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:52.160
Within Amazon, one creates an
account to do the same thing.

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:55.810
Well, GDC introduces the notion
of fleets and fleet namespaces

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:58.900
to provide that level of
resource management and tenancy

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:01.480
within a GPC instance.

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:04.460
Fleets are hardware
separated from each other,

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:08.200
allowing a single GPC instance
to serve multiple applications

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:11.020
with good security
guarantees between them.

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:13.690
And fleet namespaces,
like Amazon accounts

00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:16.120
and GCP projects,
provide isolation

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:19.470
between teams and
components of applications.

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:22.320
Policy control is built
in from the beginning.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:24.910
As we said earlier, we're
Kubernetes all the way down.

00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:29.520
So all the APIs benefit from
having Anthos policy controller

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:33.210
based on OPA gatekeeper,
and Kubernetes emission

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:36.030
control, such that one
can govern all aspects

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:38.370
of the usage, and also audit.

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:41.190
This means you could,
for example, take a VM

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:43.770
and say no VMs can ever
have the name Ray in it,

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:47.430
and we could enforce such a
policy at that granularity.

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:50.460
It's also extensible
and customizable.

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:54.030
So Kubernetes APIs allow
for custom controllers

00:09:54.030 --> 00:09:55.860
to do any sorts of things.

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:58.950
We've had customers
build controllers

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:01.200
that disallow
launching of new code

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:02.730
unless there's an
associated launch

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:04.890
process that's been approved.

00:10:04.890 --> 00:10:07.560
And Google Distributed
Cloud Hosted Mode

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:09.420
is a rich and open ecosystem.

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:11.850
Because it's built on
standard Kubernetes,

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:14.100
one could use the entire
community of software

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:16.905
available to operate
Kubernetes within GPC.

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:24.980
GDC comes with much
security and confidentiality

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:26.820
features built in.

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:30.700
A local certificate
authority backed by an HSM

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:32.600
is provided out of the box.

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:39.110
This allows GDC to meet FIPS
140-2 level 3 compliance.

00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:40.720
In addition, GDC
can be configured

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:45.230
to work with an existing HSM
provided by the customer.

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:48.590
Secrets management is provided
using the common secrets API,

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:52.710
but it's backed by the
HSM to make it secure.

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:56.760
Also, GDC benefits from
Google's investment

00:10:56.760 --> 00:11:00.480
in making GKE secure, and
all of the enhancements put

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:02.970
into GKE so far, plus
Google's contributions

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:05.440
into the Kubernetes community.

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:07.630
Identity and access
management is

00:11:07.630 --> 00:11:11.320
supported using standard
protocols like OADC and SAML

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:13.810
2.0, meaning it
can be configured

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:16.210
to work with existing
identity providers

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:18.650
that our customers have.

00:11:18.650 --> 00:11:21.470
And because it's Kubernetes
all the way down,

00:11:21.470 --> 00:11:24.830
every aspect of the system
is managed by Kubernetes role

00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:27.700
based access control.

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:30.400
If you know GCPE,
you will understand

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:35.710
that IM is it's part of every
control surface within Google

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:37.030
Cloud platform.

00:11:37.030 --> 00:11:39.970
Well within Google
Distributed Cloud,

00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:43.420
Kubernetes RBAC performs and
operates at that same level,

00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:47.320
providing fine-grained
control over all resources.

00:11:47.320 --> 00:11:51.212
Additionally, supply chain
security, a top concern,

00:11:51.212 --> 00:11:52.920
is something that
we've been working hard

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:54.900
both on the hardware
and software side.

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:57.690
We've been working with Tier
1 providers in each region,

00:11:57.690 --> 00:12:00.240
and we require strong
hardware supply chain security

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:04.180
as part of the GCD OEM process.

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:07.350
Additionally, the GKE and
our Google security teams

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:09.720
have been ensuring software
supply chain security

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:12.420
in the Kubernetes OSS
stacks for years and years.

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:14.130
And we've made
many contributions

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:17.490
to making Kubernetes a
safe, reliable system

00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:20.140
to run workloads on.

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:25.290
And lastly, everything you do
or perform on Google Distributed

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:27.840
Cloud Hosted Mode is audited.

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:31.230
And these audit logs are
compliant with the NIST 853

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:31.920
standards.

00:12:34.450 --> 00:12:37.960
Google Distributed
Cloud Hosted Mode

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:40.270
comes with a
variety of services.

00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:42.520
And because it's
built on Kubernetes,

00:12:42.520 --> 00:12:44.680
anything out in the
open source community

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:46.480
that runs on
Kubernetes will also

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:49.270
run on Google Distributed Cloud.

00:12:49.270 --> 00:12:52.660
We are also working with
third party OEM providers

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:55.720
to provide enterprise versions
of popular open source

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:59.570
packages enabling first
class support and enterprise

00:12:59.570 --> 00:13:00.070
features.

00:13:05.010 --> 00:13:07.520
Lastly, I'm going to chat
about operations and support,

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:11.385
a key concern when
operating a Cloud.

00:13:11.385 --> 00:13:13.190
Google Distributed
Cloud Hosted Mode

00:13:13.190 --> 00:13:15.200
has a few different
operational patterns

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:18.390
to support customer needs,
which Andy will cover later.

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:20.870
But importantly, the bring
up in deployment of GDC

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:25.300
will be facilitated by
Google every step of the way.

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:28.600
As mentioned earlier, all
the operational aspects

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:31.330
are provided and
operated by Google,

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:33.250
meaning that
customers do not have

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:38.020
to worry about patching and OS
upgrades, BIOS, firmware, CVE

00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:40.670
response, et cetera, et cetera.

00:13:40.670 --> 00:13:43.930
Instead, Google makes
this largely push button,

00:13:43.930 --> 00:13:46.540
or can even provide the
support and operations

00:13:46.540 --> 00:13:49.800
to do that for you.

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:52.350
Additionally, the
ability to support an air

00:13:52.350 --> 00:13:54.360
gapped deployments,
Google engineering

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:55.860
is ensuring that
every area comes

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:59.610
with full detailed support
and training resources.

00:13:59.610 --> 00:14:02.580
As an anecdote within the
engineering organization

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:05.490
for GDC, any pull
request that's submitted

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:09.210
must come with metadata for
support operations, which

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:11.160
makes engineers think
about the full lifecycle

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:13.830
up front, providing
a higher reliability

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:16.420
and operable system.

00:14:16.420 --> 00:14:19.810
And regarding
support, Google GDC

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:22.060
can be used and trusted in
air gapped environments.

00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:24.310
Google has developed
new support techniques

00:14:24.310 --> 00:14:26.830
and operational techniques
derived from Google SRE best

00:14:26.830 --> 00:14:31.060
practices, allowing GDC to
maintain a reliable SLO,

00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:32.740
while also providing
trusted user

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:37.870
support without requiring any
data to leave the GDC instance.

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:40.240
Importantly for all
operations, Google

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:43.770
is the only vendor a
customer has to call.

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:45.900
And lastly,
localized management.

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:50.150
So in addition to local UI,
local APIs, control plane,

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:53.630
everything being on as
part of the GDC instance

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:56.030
in air gapped or
other environments,

00:14:56.030 --> 00:14:57.920
documentation and
access to support

00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:00.980
is also built in,
meaning that users do not

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:05.440
need any outside access
to operate and use GDC.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:08.080
Now, in rare cases, if
the customer desires

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:09.910
faster support, we
will offer a way

00:15:09.910 --> 00:15:11.692
to send sanitized
logs, so they could

00:15:11.692 --> 00:15:13.900
be shared with the core
engineering teams, increasing

00:15:13.900 --> 00:15:15.460
support turnaround.

00:15:15.460 --> 00:15:20.200
With that, I'm going to
pass the baton back to Andy.

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:22.320
Thank you very much.

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:24.110
ANDY QIN: Thank you, Ray.

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:27.860
To sum it up, Google
Distributed Cloud Hosted Mode

00:15:27.860 --> 00:15:31.880
gives you the power of Cloud
innovation with control.

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:36.180
And it covers all
aspects of sovereignty.

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:39.870
Data is on customer premise
or any data center designated

00:15:39.870 --> 00:15:42.010
by the customer.

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:45.750
It can be operated by a customer
directly or by an operating

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:48.990
partner that has the right
credentials and the trust

00:15:48.990 --> 00:15:51.220
from the end customer.

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:53.350
Google will train
and work closely

00:15:53.350 --> 00:15:55.720
with those chosen partners
to ensure the service

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:57.190
availability.

00:15:57.190 --> 00:16:02.410
In certain scenarios, Google
can also operate the deployment

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:08.050
directly with cleared and
certified operational teams.

00:16:08.050 --> 00:16:10.120
Its build with
open API, and means

00:16:10.120 --> 00:16:12.520
a lot when customers
want to ensure

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:17.980
business continuity in any
potential black swan events.

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:20.710
And it definitely helps
to meet local government's

00:16:20.710 --> 00:16:22.330
regulatory requirements.

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:28.515
Google Distributed
Cloud Hosted Mode--

00:16:28.515 --> 00:16:33.228
RAY COLLINE: --the benefits
of Cloud delivered locally.

00:16:33.228 --> 00:16:34.770
ANDY QIN: This
concludes our session.

00:16:34.770 --> 00:16:35.910
Thank you for listening.

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:38.938
Thank you, Ray, for being
part of this conversation.

00:16:38.938 --> 00:16:40.730
RAY COLLINE: Thank you
for having me, Andy.

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:44.800
And I hope all of you have a
great rest of the time at Next.

